WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit
9: Music as Universal Language.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.580
Welcome to Unit 9, the final unit of Year 8.

3
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:17.180
This unit is called "Music as Universal Language"
because you will explore how music, performance,

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.220
and the creative arts bring people together
across cultures, languages, and backgrounds.

5
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.060
Music is one of the most powerful forms of human expression.

6
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.440
It can communicate emotions that words alone cannot capture.

7
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.300
A song can make you feel joy, sorrow, excitement,

8
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.420
or peace without you understanding a single
word of the language in which it is sung.

9
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.360
That is why we call music a universal
language: it speaks to everyone.

10
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.620
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

11
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.600
By the end of this unit,

12
00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.600
you will be able to explain what it means to call music a
universal language and discuss how the performing arts connect

13
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.180
people across cultures.

14
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:09.880
you will also read the story "the curtain never lies" and
answer comprehension and analysis questions using evidence

15
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:10.760
from the text.

16
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:18.140
you will also use thirty key vocabulary words
related to performance, creativity, emotion,

17
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.940
and integrity in your own speaking and writing.

18
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.720
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

19
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:32.190
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
havoc, deft, render, muster, deploy, gait.

20
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.860
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

21
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.700
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

22
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:52.490
The reading sequence includes Music as Universal Language:
source text 1 and Music as Universal Language: source text 2.

23
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.020
Read actively.

24
00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.320
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

25
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.540
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

26
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.540
The first language focus is Present Perfect Continuous.

27
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:18.560
What it means and why it matters The Present Perfect Continuous
tense is used to talk about actions that started in the

28
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.440
past and are still happening now,

29
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.320
or that were happening recently and have just stopped.

30
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.480
This tense emphasises the duration
and ongoing nature of the action.

31
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:35.620
It is one of the most useful tenses for describing
recent experiences, long-running activities,

32
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.180
and situations where you can see the visible
result of an activity that has been in progress.

33
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.360
For example, if your friend's...

34
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:51.840
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

35
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:58.400
In speaking, you will complete Speaking
1: Role-Play Interview with a Performer.

36
00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:04.300
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

37
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.900
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

38
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.700
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

39
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.520
You are ready to begin.

40
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:20.340
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

41
00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.200
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

42
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.480
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
